WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.880
Day and night, fluid seeps from your smallest blood vessels into nearby tissue.

2
00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:09.140
The textbook says most of it slips back in at the far end.

3
00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:11.440
Does it?

4
00:00:14.383 --> 00:00:18.043
Start with the push: pressure inside minus pressure outside.

5
00:00:19.323 --> 00:00:25.183
In resting muscle, take 18 mmHg inside and about -1 outside.

6
00:00:26.503 --> 00:00:27.543
The push is 19.

7
00:00:28.767 --> 00:00:29.987
Now the hold-back.

8
00:00:31.367 --> 00:00:36.307
Plasma proteins, mostly albumin, pull water toward the blood with about 26.

9
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:42.547
Protein just past the wall's sugary lining, the glycocalyx, pulls back with 6.

10
00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:47.750
The wall lets a little albumin through, so that difference counts at about nine

11
00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:52.350
tenths: eighteen. Push nineteen, hold-back eighteen.

12
00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:54.890
The net is one, outward.

13
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.880
Pressure falls along the capillary, from about 32 to 12.

14
00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.540
Keep the hold-back at 18.

15
00:01:03.860 --> 00:01:07.900
The arterial end nets 15 outward, the venous end 5 inward.

16
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:10.520
That's the textbook picture.

17
00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:14.243
But that sum held the outside protein fixed.

18
00:01:15.663 --> 00:01:17.863
Zoom in on one gap between lining cells.

19
00:01:19.003 --> 00:01:23.563
The glycocalyx covers its mouth like a sieve, over a thin film of fluid.

20
00:01:24.667 --> 00:01:29.167
When the vessel filters, nearly protein-free fluid streams out through the gap.

21
00:01:30.387 --> 00:01:34.927
It rinses the film, so protein there stays low, and the hold-back stays strong.

22
00:01:36.017 --> 00:01:37.537
Now reverse the stream.

23
00:01:38.797 --> 00:01:43.497
When a vessel absorbs, water leaves the film for the blood, but the protein stays

24
00:01:43.497 --> 00:01:47.497
behind. It piles up, and the hold-back weakens.

25
00:01:48.500 --> 00:01:49.980
Here is the one idea.

26
00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.200
The hold-back is set by the flow itself.

27
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.840
Filtering keeps it strong.

28
00:01:57.220 --> 00:01:59.580
Absorbing weakens it until absorption stops.

29
00:02:00.650 --> 00:02:03.830
Now the venous end, where the push is only 13.

30
00:02:03.830 --> 00:02:09.330
In the steady state, does it absorb, filter, or sit at exactly zero?

31
00:02:15.417 --> 00:02:17.697
A trickle outward, about 0.3.

32
00:02:19.017 --> 00:02:21.317
Absorbing would pile protein up and stop itself.

33
00:02:22.557 --> 00:02:26.797
If water stopped moving, protein would seep out until the hold-back vanished.

34
00:02:27.917 --> 00:02:32.757
At the arterial end, faster filtering rinses harder, so the hold-back grows.

35
00:02:34.137 --> 00:02:36.737
The net there falls from 15 to about 12.

36
00:02:38.017 --> 00:02:40.857
In this simple teaching model, every point filters.

37
00:02:42.017 --> 00:02:45.117
Plotted, the textbook line runs straight into absorption.

38
00:02:46.457 --> 00:02:50.377
The steady curve bends near 22 and flattens just above zero.

39
00:02:51.697 --> 00:02:53.217
That shape is the hockey stick.

40
00:02:54.267 --> 00:02:58.607
Single frog capillaries held at low pressure absorbed only briefly.

41
00:02:59.907 --> 00:03:03.347
Then fluid movement fell to near zero, or slight filtration.

42
00:03:04.787 --> 00:03:07.407
Nearly all this evidence is from animal vessels.

43
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:13.850
So in most tissues, the protein's pull can brake the outflow, but not turn it around

44
00:03:13.850 --> 00:03:17.730
for long. The leaked fluid needs another way home.
